VALUES ('ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 'aws-saa-practice-6', 'AWS SAA-C03 Practice Paper 6', 'aws-saa', ARRAY['VPC Endpoint Policies', 'ACM Private CA', 'AWS Backup', 'DR Strategies', 'Aurora Global Database', 'CloudFront Origin Groups', 'Savings Plans']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 6, 30);

-- ── Domain: Secure Architectures (30% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('fd38dbd3-7322-4f4c-86b4-f455ee3683c4', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 1, 'A user has an IAM identity-based policy granting full S3 access, but the target S3 bucket has a bucket policy that explicitly denies access to that user''s account. When the user attempts to access the bucket, is the request allowed or denied, and why?');
VALUES ('fd38dbd3-7322-4f4c-86b4-f455ee3683c4', '1035b50b-715f-486a-ba98-61346d69a728', 'IAM policy evaluation logic is consistent across identity-based and resource-based policies: AWS gathers every policy that applies to a given request — the calling principal''s identity-based policies, any relevant resource-based policy (like an S3 bucket policy), any SCPs (covered in Paper 3), and any permissions boundary (covered in Paper 5) — and evaluates them together. Within that combined evaluation, an explicit Deny found in ANY of those policies wins over an Allow found in any other, no matter how broad or specific either statement is, and regardless of which policy TYPE the Deny came from. This is a foundational, frequently-tested evaluation rule precisely because it''s counter-intuitive to assume "my IAM user policy grants full access" is the final word — a bucket owner can always lock out specific principals via an explicit Deny in the bucket policy even if that principal''s own identity policy is maximally permissive. Bucket policies are fully capable of denying access (not just granting it), and resource-based policies are not ignored just because an identity-based policy also exists — both are evaluated together, with explicit Deny as the deciding factor when one is present.');

VALUES ('6a38026b-87c8-4aa6-8000-bba92b03f2d4', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 2, 'A company uses a Gateway VPC Endpoint to reach S3 privately, but wants to further restrict that endpoint so that traffic through it can only reach ONE specific bucket, not any bucket in the account or elsewhere. What mechanism enforces this restriction?');
VALUES ('6a38026b-87c8-4aa6-8000-bba92b03f2d4', '7fbb83ce-f321-43c3-a07f-8c1f95eec2ce', 'A VPC endpoint policy is a distinct, resource-based policy attached directly to the endpoint resource itself (Gateway or Interface Endpoint, covered generally in Paper 5) — separate from IAM identity policies and separate from the target service''s own resource policy (like an S3 bucket policy) — that specifically scopes what the endpoint itself will permit to pass through it. Restricting a Gateway Endpoint''s policy to a single bucket ARN means even a principal with otherwise-broad S3 permissions cannot use THAT PATH to reach any other bucket, which is a genuinely useful defense-in-depth control: combined with a bucket policy requiring `aws:SourceVpce` to match the endpoint (denying access that doesn''t come through it), this creates a tight, verifiable channel where only specific traffic to a specific bucket is possible. Security groups (covered in Paper 1) attach to ENIs/resources with IP-based network traffic, not to Gateway Endpoints (which have no ENI and instead work via route table entries) — this is a genuinely common exam distinction. NACLs also operate at the subnet/IP level, not on Gateway Endpoint resource scoping.');

VALUES ('8e320165-2504-4efe-83c0-0d95c0feb0e0', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 3, 'An application needs temporary, narrowly-scoped permission to use a specific KMS key for encryption operations, granted programmatically to a specific principal for a limited purpose, without editing that key''s overall key policy every time. What KMS mechanism is designed for this kind of fine-grained, temporary permission grant?');
VALUES ('8e320165-2504-4efe-83c0-0d95c0feb0e0', '4b78cf78-4d9e-4178-b0fd-ca8cf38cd054', 'KMS grants exist specifically to solve the "temporary, narrow, programmatic" permission problem without requiring every access change to go through editing the key policy document itself (which is a heavier, slower-to-automate operation, and key policies covered generally alongside cross-account key access in Paper 1). A grant specifies a principal, the specific operations permitted (e.g., only Decrypt, not full key administration), and optional constraints, and can be created and retired programmatically — this is exactly the mechanism many AWS services use under the hood to gain temporary access to a customer-managed key on that customer''s behalf for a specific operation, without the customer needing to hand-edit their key policy for every such interaction. Security groups and NACLs (both covered in Paper 1) are network-traffic-filtering constructs entirely unrelated to KMS key-level permissions — genuinely different layers of the AWS security model, a distinction the exam tests specifically because these terms can otherwise blur together for a learner who hasn''t worked with KMS directly.');

VALUES ('322acd7d-9449-4896-9837-b061d3a6e376', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 4, 'A company needs TLS certificates for INTERNAL services (e.g., microservice-to-microservice traffic, or internal load balancers not exposed publicly) where the standard public Certificate Authority trust model doesn''t apply, but they still want centralized, managed certificate issuance rather than running their own CA infrastructure by hand. What AWS service fits this need?');
VALUES ('322acd7d-9449-4896-9837-b061d3a6e376', '023b33a0-202f-4fed-931e-edeef91cc409', 'ACM Private CA is specifically built for the "internal trust, not public trust" scenario: it lets an organization operate its own certificate authority hierarchy (root and optionally subordinate CAs) that issues certificates trusted only by systems that have been configured to trust that private root — exactly right for internal service-to-service TLS, internal load balancers, and IoT fleets, where a publicly-trusted certificate (from standard ACM, covered in Paper 4, which issues and auto-renews public certificates trusted by browsers/clients everywhere via the public CA trust chain) isn''t the right fit and would actually be the wrong tool, since public CAs generally won''t (and per public CA/Browser Forum rules, largely can''t) issue certificates for purely internal/non-publicly-resolvable names anyway. The key distinction tested here is standard public ACM (public trust, covered already) versus ACM Private CA (private/internal trust, new this paper) — genuinely different services solving different trust-model problems, not the same feature under two names. Secrets Manager (covered in Paper 2) stores and rotates secrets like database credentials, unrelated to operating a certificate authority.');

-- ── Domain: Secure Architectures (continued) ──

VALUES ('e72c8ec8-59db-4cc9-9e60-a67f433cc9a1', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 5, 'A company manages dozens of AWS accounts under AWS Organizations and wants a security baseline where NO account can use any AWS service by default — each account must be explicitly granted access only to the specific services it actually needs, rather than starting from full access and trying to remove what''s unwanted. What SCP strategy achieves this?');
VALUES ('e72c8ec8-59db-4cc9-9e60-a67f433cc9a1', '0b503296-e0e4-41bf-9c29-5f05f9bda13b', 'By default, every AWS Organizations account/OU has the `FullAWSAccess` SCP attached, which explicitly allows everything — meaning SCPs (covered generally, with the deny-list pattern of explicitly denying specific dangerous actions, in Paper 3) start from an "everything permitted, then subtract" baseline unless changed. To flip that to "nothing permitted unless explicitly allowed" (an allow-list strategy), an organization detaches or replaces that default policy with a custom SCP granting Allow statements ONLY for the specific services each OU/account genuinely needs — since SCPs never grant permissions on their own (they only set the maximum boundary an account''s own IAM policies can grant within, a ceiling, not a grant), an allow-list SCP effectively means "nothing is usable in this account unless both the SCP AND a local IAM policy allow it," a materially stronger security baseline than a deny-list approach for organizations that know their accounts'' full needs are narrow and well-defined. This is a genuinely more restrictive, more deliberate posture than the default and than most deny-list SCP usage, which is exactly why it''s the correct fit for the "no service by default" requirement in this scenario — a security group operates at the network-traffic layer per Paper 1, entirely unrelated to organization-wide service-level restriction.');

VALUES ('95be5ad1-d36f-431f-9004-c305311799a4', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 6, 'A company grants a third-party SaaS vendor an IAM role in its AWS account (via a cross-account trust policy) so the vendor can access specific resources on the company''s behalf. The company is aware of the "confused deputy" risk — where a different, unrelated third party could trick the vendor into misusing that same role against the company''s account. What specific trust-policy condition directly mitigates this risk?');
VALUES ('95be5ad1-d36f-431f-9004-c305311799a4', '225e42c5-b2ac-45f8-bf7f-87439a1bed48', 'The confused-deputy problem here specifically arises because a role''s ARN alone isn''t secret — if a malicious third party learns that ARN (e.g., by working with the same vendor for a different, unrelated customer), they could potentially trick the vendor''s own systems into assuming the company''s role on the malicious party''s behalf, since the vendor''s systems have no way to distinguish "this AssumeRole request is legitimately for the company we intended" from "this is a different customer''s attempt to reach into the company''s account through us." Requiring a specific, unique `sts:ExternalId` value (known only to the company and that specific vendor relationship, and included as a condition in the trust policy) closes this gap directly: the vendor''s systems must supply that exact external ID on every AssumeRole call for it to succeed, so a different, unrelated party attempting to trigger the same role assumption through the vendor — without knowing that specific external ID — is denied, even with a technically-valid role ARN. This connects directly to STS AssumeRole covered generally in Paper 3, refining it with the specific condition that addresses this specific, real, well-documented cross-account risk pattern (which is genuinely mitigable, not an unavoidable inherent risk) — a security group or NACL, both network-traffic-layer constructs from Paper 1, has no bearing on STS API-level trust conditions.');

-- ── Domain: Resilient Architectures (26% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('176dd669-fbbb-4dae-9fc6-410188b870a4', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 7, 'A company wants a Route 53 health check on its primary application endpoint that only reports healthy if BOTH its web tier AND its database tier are individually healthy — not just the web tier responding on its own. What Route 53 health check feature supports combining multiple individual health checks into one overall status?');
VALUES ('176dd669-fbbb-4dae-9fc6-410188b870a4', '5a8a70cd-85ec-4e29-a1b0-21d305e03667', 'A calculated health check is Route 53''s specific mechanism for exactly this "is the whole system healthy, not just one piece of it" requirement — rather than monitoring an endpoint directly, a calculated health check instead monitors the STATUS of other, already-configured child health checks (which might each individually monitor the web tier, the database tier, or any other component) and derives its own healthy/unhealthy status from a configurable combination rule across those children. This is directly useful for failover routing policies (Route 53 failover covered generally in Paper 2, and latency/multi-value routing in Papers 4 and 5) where the routing decision needs to reflect true end-to-end application health rather than just "is the load balancer responding," since a web tier can appear healthy on its own while the database tier behind it has actually failed. CloudWatch alarms CAN also feed into a Route 53 health check''s status as a data source, but the "combine multiple existing checks into one derived status" capability specifically is the calculated health check feature, not a CloudWatch-exclusive capability as one distractor claims. Route 53 health checks are not limited to monitoring only one endpoint in isolation — calculated health checks are a real, standard feature specifically because monitoring only one component in a multi-tier application is often an incomplete picture.');

VALUES ('0cbc57c1-0788-47a6-8eb8-2d7a16e65387', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 8, 'For an RDS database, a company compares two resilience options: (1) Multi-AZ deployment with automatic failover to a synchronously-replicated standby, versus (2) manually promoting an asynchronously-replicated read replica to become the new primary. Which of these generally provides faster, more automatic recovery, and why?');
VALUES ('0cbc57c1-0788-47a6-8eb8-2d7a16e65387', '11773c7b-2622-4554-838a-85195f9dd733', 'RDS Multi-AZ (covered generally in Paper 1) maintains a standby replica kept in SYNCHRONOUS lock-step with the primary specifically so that, upon a detected failure, RDS can automatically redirect the database''s endpoint to that standby with no manual step and (because replication was synchronous) essentially no data loss for committed transactions — this automatic detection-and-redirect process is what typically completes in roughly one to two minutes. A read replica (covered generally alongside replica lag in Paper 4), by contrast, uses ASYNCHRONOUS replication — meaning there is normally some lag between what''s written to the primary and what''s reflected on the replica — and promoting it to become a new writable primary is a deliberate, manually (or scripted) triggered action, not an automatic RDS-managed failover; because of that replication lag, the promoted replica could be missing the very latest transactions that hadn''t yet replicated at the moment of the original failure. This distinction — automatic + synchronous + minimal data loss (Multi-AZ) versus manual + asynchronous + possible data loss (read replica promotion) — is a core, frequently-tested RDS resilience concept, and Multi-AZ failover is not literally instantaneous nor unsupported, contrary to the distractor claims.');

VALUES ('5d6a1293-17f8-4046-90d4-869b359f8c90', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 9, 'A company uses several different AWS services — EBS volumes, RDS databases, DynamoDB tables, and EFS file systems — and wants ONE centralized place to define backup schedules, retention policies, and cross-region backup copies that apply consistently across all of them, rather than configuring backups separately in each individual service''s own console.');
VALUES ('5d6a1293-17f8-4046-90d4-869b359f8c90', 'f63ea725-fe3f-48d4-ba3b-e1096ad3c709', 'AWS Backup is specifically the centralized, cross-service backup management layer built for exactly this need — rather than configuring EBS snapshot lifecycle (covered generally in Paper 4), RDS automated backups/PITR (covered in Paper 2), DynamoDB backups, and EFS backups each independently in their own separate consoles with potentially inconsistent schedules and retention, AWS Backup lets an organization define ONE backup plan (schedule, retention tiers, transition to cold storage, and cross-region or cross-account copy for disaster recovery) and apply it consistently via tags or direct resource assignment across all of those supported services at once. This directly reduces the operational risk of one service quietly having a weaker or misconfigured backup policy than the others, a genuinely common real-world gap, and provides a single place to audit backup compliance across an entire estate. AWS Config (covered in Paper 3) audits resource CONFIGURATION compliance over time, and Trusted Advisor (covered in Paper 4) surfaces cost/security/performance recommendations — neither is the centralized backup-policy-management service itself, though Config could separately be used to verify AWS Backup policies are actually applied.');

VALUES ('7d56df9b-f65f-4db6-b12c-0176dbc8d727', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 10, 'A company is choosing a disaster recovery strategy and wants the fastest possible recovery time (near-zero RTO/RPO) by keeping a full-scale, continuously-running duplicate of production infrastructure in a second region at all times, ready to take over traffic instantly. Which standard DR strategy tier does this describe, and what is its main trade-off compared to a "pilot light" approach?');
VALUES ('7d56df9b-f65f-4db6-b12c-0176dbc8d727', 'd354e702-f59e-4d43-9895-337e62cd8b18', 'The standard AWS DR strategy spectrum runs from cheapest/slowest to most-expensive/fastest: backup and restore (data backed up, infrastructure rebuilt from scratch only when needed — slowest, cheapest), pilot light (only the smallest critical core, often just a replicated database, kept running — the rest of the stack is provisioned and scaled up only during an actual disaster), warm standby (a scaled-DOWN but fully functional duplicate environment already running, scaled up during a disaster), and multi-site active/active (a full-scale, continuously-running duplicate already handling live traffic, or ready to instantly take over all of it) — RTO/RPO improve at each tier, at directly increasing cost, since more infrastructure is running continuously rather than only during an actual disaster event. Multi-site active/active is specifically the tier matching "near-zero RTO/RPO, full-scale, always running" — its clear, real trade-off against pilot light (a much smaller continuously-running footprint) is cost: paying for a fully duplicated, continuously-running environment around the clock, most of the time doing nothing but standing by, versus only scaling that cost up when a disaster genuinely occurs. Backup and restore is the OPPOSITE end of this spectrum from what''s described, and cost genuinely does vary significantly across these tiers — a real, standard trade-off the exam tests directly.');

VALUES ('8a3f965f-d265-4060-b602-adaa718e414a', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 11, 'A company running critical on-premises servers wants near-continuous, block-level replication of those servers to AWS, so that in a disaster they can launch fully-recovered, ready-to-use EC2 instances from that replicated data within minutes, without maintaining a permanently-running duplicate environment in AWS the whole time. What AWS service is purpose-built for this kind of continuous, low-RPO replication-based recovery?');
VALUES ('8a3f965f-d265-4060-b602-adaa718e414a', 'c21b663e-45a8-4ff9-90f8-4b38aa8c612c', 'AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS) is specifically purpose-built for this "continuous replication, minimal ongoing cost, fast recovery" pattern — an agent on the source server continuously replicates changes at the block level into a low-cost staging area in AWS (using minimal compute resources while idle, not full-sized running instances), and when a disaster is actually declared, DRS launches fully-provisioned, correctly-sized recovery instances from that continuously up-to-date replicated data, typically within minutes — this maps directly onto the "pilot light"-to-"warm standby" DR strategy spectrum covered in the prior question, giving low ongoing cost with a recovery point close to real-time. This is a genuinely distinct, purpose-built DR service, different from the Snow Family (physical data-transfer devices for bulk migration, covered in Paper 3, not continuous replication) and from Trusted Advisor (recommendations, covered in Paper 4, not a replication/recovery execution service) — both are common category-confusion distractors against a specialized, real DR-focused service.');

-- ── Domain: High-Performing Architectures (24% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('e1cf9ce1-93d6-4af1-8636-2d2348945609', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 12, 'An application running on EC2 performs heavy, sustained disk I/O to attached EBS volumes, and the team notices that under load, EBS throughput is being constrained by competing for the same network bandwidth the instance also uses for its regular application traffic. What EC2 feature specifically addresses this by giving EBS traffic its own dedicated bandwidth, separate from general network traffic?');
VALUES ('e1cf9ce1-93d6-4af1-8636-2d2348945609', '7dffcc83-1a02-4b8a-805c-b6ebe58e3889', 'EBS-optimized instances exist specifically to solve this exact contention problem — on a non-optimized configuration, EBS traffic and the instance''s general network traffic (application requests, inter-service calls) can compete for the same shared network interface bandwidth, meaning a burst of heavy disk I/O can degrade application network performance and vice versa. Enabling (or, on many current-generation instance types, simply having by default) EBS optimization provisions dedicated, separate throughput specifically for EBS traffic, so the two workloads don''t interfere with each other — directly relevant to sustained, I/O-heavy workloads like databases or heavy logging/batch-processing applications on EC2, and complementary to choosing an appropriately-provisioned EBS volume type itself (io2/gp3, covered generally in Papers 1 and elsewhere) since a fast volume doesn''t help if the instance-level network path to reach it is the actual bottleneck. Placement groups (covered in Paper 3) affect inter-instance network placement/latency, not EBS-versus-general bandwidth separation, and purchasing a Reserved Instance (a billing commitment, covered generally in Paper 1) has no effect on this network architecture at all — this is not a universal, zero-configuration default across every instance type, contrary to the last distractor.');

VALUES ('ba48d353-1733-463f-8d44-ac1de819b966', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 13, 'A company has users worldwide uploading large files directly to a single S3 bucket in one region, and users far from that region experience slow upload speeds due to the distance the data must travel over the public internet. What S3 feature routes uploads through nearby CloudFront edge locations over an optimized network path to speed this up, without requiring the bucket itself to move or be replicated?');
VALUES ('ba48d353-1733-463f-8d44-ac1de819b966', 'a312fc8c-9b6d-4bc6-85bb-aff4fc97bbe6', 'S3 Transfer Acceleration solves exactly this "distant user, slow public-internet path to a single bucket" problem by using CloudFront''s globally distributed edge locations as entry points — once enabled on a bucket, uploads/downloads go to the nearest edge location first (typically a much shorter, faster public-internet hop for the end user), and from there travel over Amazon''s own private, optimized backbone network to the bucket''s actual region, rather than the public internet the entire distance. This is a genuinely different mechanism from CloudFront''s general content-caching role (covered in Paper 1) — Transfer Acceleration is specifically about accelerating the PATH to S3 (uploads and non-cacheable transfers included), not about caching content at the edge for repeated downloads. S3 Object Lock (covered in Paper 5) is a WORM/compliance retention feature, and S3 Select (covered in Paper 3) retrieves a subset of an object''s data via a query — both entirely unrelated to transfer speed/routing, and genuinely common category-confusion distractors against a real, purpose-built acceleration feature.');

VALUES ('1951cc3e-462f-4201-864b-c3542651a048', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 14, 'A company''s Lambda function experiences noticeable latency spikes for the first request after a period of inactivity, caused by the time needed to initialize a new execution environment (a "cold start"), which is unacceptable for a latency-sensitive, customer-facing API. What Lambda feature keeps a specified number of execution environments pre-initialized and ready to respond instantly, eliminating this cold-start delay for those requests?');
VALUES ('1951cc3e-462f-4201-864b-c3542651a048', 'b91e8381-b6a8-417f-a173-b0caf2447c05', 'Provisioned concurrency directly targets the cold-start problem (covered generally, alongside its causes, in Paper 4) by keeping a configured number of execution environments already initialized and idle-but-ready at all times, so requests routed to one of those pre-warmed environments skip the initialization delay entirely and respond with consistently low latency — the direct trade-off is cost, since AWS charges for provisioned concurrency capacity whether or not it''s actively handling a request at any given moment, unlike standard on-demand Lambda invocations, which only incur cost during actual execution. This is a meaningfully different concept from RESERVED concurrency (a related-sounding but distinct Lambda setting that caps/reserves how much concurrent execution capacity a function is guaranteed or limited to — a concurrency-limiting mechanism, not a cold-start-eliminating one) — a genuinely common exam confusion given the similar names, worth being precise about. Lambda@Edge (running Lambda functions at CloudFront edge locations) is a different capability entirely, unrelated to warming execution environments for cold-start elimination — and cold-start latency is a real, well-documented, and directly mitigable characteristic, not an unfixable constant.');

VALUES ('1396cce2-f898-42d5-9ce2-1906b2fafb2f', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 15, 'A company runs an Aurora database with a primary write region and wants read access from application servers in three other AWS regions worldwide, with typical cross-region replication lag under one second and the ability to promote a secondary region to full read/write within about a minute if the primary region fails entirely. What Aurora feature is purpose-built for this?');
VALUES ('1396cce2-f898-42d5-9ce2-1906b2fafb2f', '4a2b4851-dc87-4fb4-b60f-8220e095a676', 'Aurora Global Database is specifically purpose-built for exactly this multi-region, low-latency-reads-plus-fast-DR-failover requirement — it uses dedicated replication infrastructure (distinct from Aurora''s standard within-region storage-layer replication, and from ordinary cross-region read replicas, which typically have materially higher lag) to keep secondary regions''s data current with typical lag under one second, giving genuinely low-latency local reads to application servers in each of those regions rather than round-tripping every read back to the primary region. In a primary-region failure, a secondary region can be promoted to take over full read/write duties in roughly a minute, which is a meaningfully different, more purpose-built DR/global-read solution than general Aurora (covered in Paper 3) or Aurora Serverless v2 (covered in Paper 5, which addresses auto-scaling database capacity within a single deployment, not cross-region replication). DynamoDB Global Tables (covered in Paper 3) is DynamoDB''s OWN separate multi-region replication feature, entirely unrelated to and incompatible with an Aurora relational cluster — a genuinely common cross-service-confusion distractor given the similar "Global" naming across two different AWS database products.');

VALUES ('82280465-d280-472f-9b00-77612d712c96', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 16, 'A company serves static content through CloudFront with an S3 bucket as the origin, and wants to configure a SECOND origin (a backup S3 bucket in another region) that CloudFront automatically switches to if the primary origin returns errors, without requiring any DNS change or manual intervention. What CloudFront feature supports this automatic origin failover?');
VALUES ('82280465-d280-472f-9b00-77612d712c96', '3d44b290-5147-4d70-b800-4383daf7dff7', 'CloudFront origin groups are specifically the feature for this exact need — pairing a primary and a secondary origin under one distribution configuration, with CloudFront itself automatically detecting when the primary origin returns a status code configured as a failure trigger (e.g., 5xx errors) and routing that request (and subsequent ones, per the configured behavior) to the secondary origin instead — all handled within CloudFront''s own request routing, with no DNS-level change or external health-check service required, unlike Route 53 failover routing (covered in Paper 2, which operates at the DNS layer, redirecting where a domain name resolves to based on health checks — a different mechanism operating at a different layer than CloudFront''s own internal origin failover). Origin Shield (covered in Paper 5) is a caching-layer optimization reducing origin load via an additional caching tier, not a failover mechanism between two distinct origins. CloudFront distributions genuinely do support configuring multiple origins (including origin groups) — this is a real, standard resilience feature, not a hard single-origin limitation.');

-- ── Domain: Cost-Optimized Architectures (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('642a9f17-77ad-44fb-8860-9ea86d06c577', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 17, 'A company has an S3 bucket with unpredictable, evolving access patterns across millions of objects and wants AWS to analyze actual access frequency over time and recommend which objects would benefit from moving to a cheaper storage class, rather than the company having to guess at lifecycle rules upfront. What S3 feature provides this data-driven recommendation?');
VALUES ('642a9f17-77ad-44fb-8860-9ea86d06c577', '9c555dfe-1ac6-4c31-8100-1aca834fb695', 'S3 Storage Class Analysis is specifically an OBSERVATIONAL, recommendation-generating tool — it watches real access patterns over an observation period and surfaces data-driven suggestions (e.g., "this set of objects hasn''t been accessed in 45+ days on average, consider transitioning to Standard-IA") for a human or automated process to then act on, typically by adjusting a lifecycle policy (covered generally in Paper 2). This is a genuinely different mechanism from S3 Intelligent-Tiering (covered in Paper 1) — Intelligent-Tiering AUTOMATICALLY moves individual objects between access tiers based on observed usage with no manual lifecycle-rule action required at all, making it a fully automated storage class itself, while Storage Class Analysis is a recommendation/reporting tool that still requires a separate lifecycle rule to actually be created based on its findings — related in purpose (both respond to real access patterns) but structurally distinct, a genuine and testable distinction. Trusted Advisor (covered in Paper 4) gives broader account-level cost/security/performance recommendations, not S3 object-level access-pattern analysis specifically.');

VALUES ('48b4abcc-268b-4148-9c0c-ba4c4fcf04fc', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 18, 'A company wants to commit to Reserved Instance pricing for steady-state EC2 usage but anticipates its instance family and size needs may change over the next two years as the application evolves. Which RI type allows exchanging the reservation for a different instance family, size, OS, or tenancy during its term, and what does the OTHER RI type trade in exchange for lacking that flexibility?');
VALUES ('48b4abcc-268b-4148-9c0c-ba4c4fcf04fc', '8b22f671-fa1f-40ae-b525-b206036c3dcb', 'Standard and Convertible RIs represent a genuine, directly-testable trade-off between commitment flexibility and discount size, both building on the general Reserved Instance concept covered in Paper 4 (RI-vs-Savings-Plans comparison): Standard RIs offer the LARGER maximum discount off On-Demand pricing but lock the reservation to its originally-specified instance family, size, OS, and tenancy for the full term, with no ability to exchange it if needs change (though they CAN still be sold on the RI Marketplace, or have limited size-flexibility within the same instance family in some cases, distinct from a full cross-family exchange). Convertible RIs sacrifice some of that maximum discount in exchange for the ability to exchange the reservation for a different configuration — different instance family, size, OS, or tenancy — during the term, directly suited to a company anticipating its needs may genuinely change, like the scenario described. This is the reverse of one distractor''s claim, and Reserved Instances genuinely do support exchange/modification in specific, real ways depending on type — not an absolute, unconditional lock across both types.');

VALUES ('65a34c27-6ad9-4888-b911-6349d1980e98', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 19, 'A company is choosing between two Savings Plan types: Compute Savings Plans and EC2 Instance Savings Plans. Both provide a discount in exchange for a one- or three-year usage commitment, but they differ in flexibility. What is the key difference between them?');
VALUES ('65a34c27-6ad9-4888-b911-6349d1980e98', 'b9c82c78-4c39-4516-ad9a-03f6536c4b1e', 'This refines the general Savings Plans concept (covered alongside RI-vs-SP comparison in Paper 4) with the specific distinction between its two sub-types: Compute Savings Plans are the MOST flexible option, applying automatically to usage across EC2 (regardless of instance family, size, OS, tenancy, or region), AWS Fargate, and AWS Lambda, all under one commitment — ideal for an organization whose compute mix or instance needs may shift over the commitment term, or that uses a mix of EC2 and serverless compute — but that broad flexibility comes at a somewhat lower maximum discount than the narrower option. EC2 Instance Savings Plans commit specifically to a chosen instance family WITHIN a specific region (though still flexible across instance size, OS, and tenancy within that family), offering a higher maximum discount in exchange for that reduced scope — directly analogous to the Standard-vs-Convertible RI flexibility/discount trade-off covered in the prior question, but for the Savings Plans mechanism specifically. Savings Plans (specifically Compute Savings Plans) genuinely do extend to Fargate and Lambda usage, not just EC2 — a meaningful, real capability distinguishing Savings Plans generally from Reserved Instances, which apply only to specific services like EC2, RDS, or ElastiCache.');

VALUES ('87a05c18-1633-4d6e-9c7b-90bfdb24abef', 'ae5e9e35-6c53-4d23-afd8-c02e7ad36198', 20, 'A company runs EC2 instances in a private subnet that regularly download software updates and access AWS APIs (like S3) through a NAT Gateway, incurring per-GB NAT Gateway data processing charges for that traffic. For traffic destined specifically to AWS services like S3, what change would reduce or eliminate those NAT Gateway data processing charges for that portion of the traffic?');
VALUES ('87a05c18-1633-4d6e-9c7b-90bfdb24abef', '9a9a7cee-68a3-4a72-be16-3fbc64aed0b6', 'This connects directly to the Gateway VPC Endpoint concept covered earlier this paper (question 2) from a cost-optimization angle rather than a security angle: without a Gateway Endpoint, traffic from a private subnet to S3 has to route out through the NAT Gateway (incurring its per-GB data processing charge, covered generally in Paper 1) even though the traffic''s ultimate destination is another AWS service, not the actual public internet. Adding a Gateway Endpoint for S3 (or DynamoDB, the two services Gateway Endpoints support) creates a route directly to that service within AWS''s own network, entirely bypassing the NAT Gateway for that specific traffic — and critically, Gateway Endpoints themselves have no additional hourly charge or data processing fee, making this a genuinely meaningful, essentially free cost optimization for any workload with substantial S3 or DynamoDB traffic currently routing through a NAT Gateway. A Reserved Instance (covered in Paper 1) is an EC2 billing commitment with no relationship to NAT Gateway data processing charges, and S3 Transfer Acceleration (covered earlier this paper) optimizes upload/download speed via CloudFront edge locations, not NAT Gateway routing/cost — this is a real, common, and meaningful cost-optimization pattern, not a fixed, unavoidable charge.');

